# Architecture Decisions

## Authentication Does Not Select the Role

The login experience does not establish authority by asking the user to
select a role.

The platform resolves the authenticated actor from persisted application
records.

The authority model is:

```text
Supabase Auth
    ↓
Application User
    ↓
Platform Membership
OR
Organization Membership
    ↓
Role Context
```

A client-selected role must never be treated as proof of authority.

---

## Platform Super Admin Precedence

Platform Super Admin authority exists above the Organization hierarchy.

A Platform Super Admin may also have Organization Membership without being
forced into the Organization-level entry experience.

The current entry resolver therefore evaluates Platform Super Admin
membership before Organization-level role context.

---

## Current Entry Context

`CurrentEntryContext` is the canonical server-side entry boundary.

It establishes:

- authenticated Application User
- Entry Actor
- Organization context where applicable

The `/entry` route consumes this context instead of independently rebuilding
identity and authority resolution.

This prevents multiple competing entry-resolution paths.

---

## Authentication vs Authorization

Authentication answers:

> Who is this user?

Authorization answers:

> What is this user allowed to access or modify?

This milestone establishes authentication and role-aware product entry.

It does not constitute complete production authorization.

Production authorization and RLS remain separate work.

